To create a simulation of a phishing attack for educational purposes, follow these steps:

### Project Overview
The goal is to develop a non-harmful simulation that mimics a real phishing attack to study user responses. The simulation will be a simple HTML webpage designed to look like a legitimate login page from a known service, such as a bank. It will include elements of urgency and a form to collect information, but it will not capture any real data or harm the system.

### Tools and Technologies
- **HTML (HyperText Markup Language):** Structure the page content.
- **CSS (Cascading Style Sheets):** Style the page to look legitimate.
- **JavaScript:** Add interactivity for form handling and redirection.

### Steps to Create the Phishing Simulation

1. **Create the HTML Structure:**
   - Use a standard HTML template with a head and body section.
   - Include a title, a header with a legitimate-looking logo, and a message.
   - Add a form with fields for username, password, and other personal information (e.g., Social Security Number, full name, email, phone number).

2. **Styling with CSS:**
   - Choose a color scheme similar to a well-known company (e.g., blue for banks).
   - Use fonts like Arial or Times New Roman for a professional look.
   - Ensure the layout is clean and professional, avoiding flashy colors or designs.

3. **Urgency and Trust Indicators:**
   - Use a warning message such as "Your Account Has Been Compromised" or "Please Reverify Your Credentials."
   - Include elements like a padlock icon or "Secure Connection" indicators to build trust.

4. **Form Handling:**
   - Use JavaScript to simulate form submission.
   - Upon form submission, display a success message (e.g., "Login successful. Redirecting to the homepage.") and redirect the user to another page or just show a thank you message.

5. **Legal and Ethical Considerations:**
   - Ensure the simulation is only used for educational purposes and does not collect real user data.
   - Test the simulation on a safe, controlled environment, such as a local server or a test domain.

6. **Testing:**
   - Conduct the simulation on a test network where users can interact without real consequences.
   - Analyze the responses to understand how users recognize and respond to the phishing attempt.

### Example Code

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Secure Login Portal</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            background-color: #f0f2f5;
            margin: 0;
            padding: 0;
        }
        .container {
            max-width: 400px;
            margin: 100px auto;
            padding: 20px;
            background-color: white;
            border-radius: 4px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
        }
        .logo {
            text-align: center;
            margin-bottom: 30px;
        }
        .logo img {
            max-width: 200px;
        }
        .message {
            color: #333;
            margin-bottom: 30px;
        }
        .form-group {
            margin-bottom: 15px;
        }
        label {
            display: block;
            margin-bottom: 5px;
            color: #666;
        }
        input {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 8px;
            border: 1px solid #ddd;
            border-radius: 4px;
            box-sizing: border-box;
        }
        button {
            background-color: #007bff;
            color: white;
            padding: 10px 20px;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 4px;
            cursor: pointer;
            width: 100%;
        }
        button:hover {
            background-color: #0056b3;
        }
        .success-message {
            color: #28a745;
            margin-top: 20px;
            text-align: center;
            font-weight: bold;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="logo">
            <img src="https://example.com/logo.png" alt="Secure Bank Logo">
        </div>
        <div class="message">
            <h2>Your Account Has Been Compromised</h2>
            <p>Please reverify your credentials to secure your account.</p>
        </div>
        <form id="loginForm">
            <div class="form-group">
                <label for="username">Username</label>
                <input type="text" id="username" required>
            </div>
            <div class="form-group">
                <label for="password">Password</label>
                <input type="password" id="password" required>
            </div>
            <div class="form-group">
                <label for="ssn">Social Security Number</label>
                <input type="text" id="ssn" required>
            </div>
            <div class="form-group">
                <label for="name">Full Name</label>
                <input type="text" id="name" required>
            </div>
            <div class="form-group">
                <label for="email">Email Address</label>
                <input type="email" id="email" required>
            </div>
            <div class="form-group">
                <label for="phone">Phone Number</label>
                <input type="tel" id="phone" required>
            </div>
            <button type="submit">Login</button>
        </form>
        <div class="success-message" id="successMessage">
            Login successful. Redirecting to the homepage...
        </div>
    </div>

    <script>
        document.getElementById('loginForm').addEventListener('submit', function(e) {
            e.preventDefault();
            const formData = new FormData(this);
            // In a real scenario, this would send data to a server
            // For simulation, just show the success message
            document.getElementById('successMessage').style.display = 'block';
            this.reset();
            setTimeout(function() {
                window.location.href = 'https://www.your-actual-website.com';
            }, 2000);
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation

- **HTML Structure:** The page includes a header with a logo, an alert message, and a form with fields for personal information.
- **Styling:** The design uses a professional color scheme and layout to mimic a legitimate login page.
- **Urgency:** The message creates a sense of urgency, encouraging users to act quickly.
- **Form Handling:** Upon submission, the form displays a success message and redirects the user, simulating a secure login process.

This simulation can be tested on a safe, controlled environment to observe user responses and improve awareness and security measures.<｜end▁of▁sentence｜>

